  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "A body of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body\u2019s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$",
  "worked_solution": "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=12\\cdot 9.8=117.6\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=117.6-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 117.6-kv & =12a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body\u2019s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}117.6-10k & =12\\cdot 6.3 \\\\ 117.6-10k & =75.6 \\\\ -10k & =-42 \\\\ k & =4.2\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=117.6-4.2v.\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n117.6-4.2{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,\u27f9\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{117.6}{4.2}=28\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=28\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
